--- !u!1001 &545711847822173931
PrefabInstance:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  serializedVersion: 2
  m_Modification:
    serializedVersion: 3
    m_TransformParent: {fileID: 7831597305242322470}
    m_Modifications:
    - target: {fileID: 2116517601969916342, guid: 183e809bca3827e41a67b7345a91f0c2, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: 'Sonic the Hedgehog was made to be fast to demonstrate the power of
        the SEGA Genesis.


        The 8-bit era''s visuals struggled with technological
        limitations at the time, such as sprite, sound, and colour limitations, low
        storage space, and so on. However, it would be succeeded and 2D graphics
        would greatly improve with the onset of the 16 bit era.


        New hardware,
        such as the SEGA Genesis and SNES in 1989, enabled complicated visual techniques.
        For instance, the SEGA Genesis had 64KB of ram, allowed a 512 color palette
        and could display 61 colors and 80 sprites on screen at once, more than doubly-powerful
        as the NES. Its flagship game, Sonic the Hedgehog (1993) demonstrated the
        SEGA Genesis processing levels in full glory.

'
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 2116517601969916342, guid: 183e809bca3827e41a67b7345a91f0c2, type: 3}
